{
  "analysis": "Control this round = last kept plan (25/15/10 default, mAP 0.6154). Evidence so far: (1) inclusion AP50 is flat ~0.38-0.42 across 30-65 cumulative synthetics (30->0.357, 55->0.421, 60->0.379, 65->0.418), i.e. inclusion is saturating; (2) downscaling inclusion hurt (0.326) while prefer_small at default scale matched/beaten it (0.418) at identical allocation; (3) shifting 5 imgs inclusion->patches raised mAP (0.603->0.615); (4) scratches needs >=10. Miss analysis: ALL classes miss small instances (median missed 33/53/40px vs train medians 90/85/73px; low-size-tertile recall worst for all). So: C1 stacks the confirmed prefer_small inclusion lever on the best allocation; C2 tests whether prefer_small generalizes to patches/scratches; C3 tests inclusion saturation by shifting 5 more imgs to patches. No downscaling, scratches held at 10, no untested pools/overlap.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hypothesis": "Stack the confirmed inclusion lever on the best allocation: prefer_small sources for inclusion (0.418 vs 0.326 for downscale at identical allocation, and >= uniform at comparable allocations) at the proven 25/15/10 split should lift inclusion AP50 above 0.421 while patches/scratches stay at their best levels. Confirmed if mAP50 > 0.6154 with inclusion AP50 >= 0.42 and no class dropping >0.02.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 25, "patches": 15, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "prefer_small", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Generalize the small-source lever: missed instances are smaller than the train median for every class (33 vs 90, 53 vs 85, 40 vs 73px) and low-size-tertile recall is worst for all, so prefer_small for patches and scratches too (same 25/15/10 allocation) should raise or hold their AP50 and beat C1 on mAP50. Confirmed if mAP50 >= C1 with patches AP50 >= 0.74 and scratches AP50 >= 0.67.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 25, "patches": 15, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "prefer_small", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "prefer_small", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "prefer_small", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Inclusion saturation: inclusion AP50 is flat ~0.38-0.42 from 30 to 65 cumulative synthetics, so shifting 5 more images from inclusion to patches (20/20/10, keeping prefer_small for inclusion) should push patches AP50 toward >=0.75 (it gained 0.708->0.745 going 10->15) without dropping inclusion below 0.40, beating C1 on mAP50. Confirmed if mAP50 > C1 with patches AP50 >= 0.75 and inclusion AP50 >= 0.40.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 20, "patches": 20, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "prefer_small", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "C3 (20/20/10, prefer_small inclusion) is the clear winner: mAP50 0.6367, beating C2 (0.6227) by 0.014 (>0.005 noise) and also having the best worst-class AP50 (0.4065). Hypothesis checks: C1 (stack prefer_small inclusion on 25/15/10) REFUTED — inclusion fell to 0.376, so the R2 'prefer_small inclusion' lever was not robust. C2 (prefer_small all classes) PARTIALLY confirmed — patches (0.772) and scratches (0.741) rose, but inclusion collapsed to 0.355. C3 (inclusion saturation) CONFIRMED — dropping inclusion 25→20 actually RAISED inclusion AP50 (0.4065 vs 0.355-0.376) and lifted scratches to 0.758, proving inclusion is saturated and the budget is better spent on patches/scratches. C4 control (0.6078) lost to C3.",
  "choice": 3,
  "lesson": "R3 (final): inclusion is SATURATED — 20 synthetics gave inclusion AP50 0.407, better than 25 (0.355-0.376), so stop feeding inclusion and shift budget to patches/scratches; the winning final model is 20/20/10 (mAP50 0.6367). prefer_small for inclusion is NOT a reliable lever (flat 0.355-0.407, noisy), while prefer_small clearly helped patches (0.772) and roughly matched uniform for scratches (0.741 vs 0.758) — the small-source lever is class-specific and weak, so allocation balance is the dominant lever. If more rounds existed: hold inclusion ~20, push patches/scratches, and re-test prefer_small on patches only."
}
